description:
Related to docs/plan/plan.md ADR-001 (2026-07-20), but independently shippable regardless of when/whether that consolidation lands.

docker-app/nginx.conf and docker-app/nginx.conf.template both have:

location ~* \.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif|ico|css|js|woff|woff2|ttf|svg|eot|otf|wasm)$ {
    expires 1y;
    add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable";
}

This regex matches every .js/.css file by extension, not just the content-hashed webpack outputs (main.[hash].js, vendors.[hash].js, runtime.[hash].js). It also catches:
- docker-app/public/app.js -- unhashed, contains actual app logic
- docker-app/public/config.js -- regenerated per-container-start by docker-app/entrypoint.sh (injects runtime APP_PREFIX config)
- docker-app/public/styles.css -- unhashed

Confirmed live 2026-07-20: curl -sI https://face.jedarden.com/app.js returns "cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable". Any future redeploy that changes app.js or config.js content without renaming the file will not reach a returning visitors browser (or Cloudflares edge cache) for up to a year.

Fix: narrow the immutable-cache location block to only match the hashed webpack output filenames (they contain a content hash segment, e.g. a pattern like \.[0-9a-f]{8,20}\.(js|css)(\.map)?$, or serve them from a dedicated /_assets/ subpath and match on the path prefix instead of extension). Give config.js and any unhashed .js/.css/.html files a short-TTL or no-cache policy (e.g. Cache-Control: no-cache, so ETag/If-None-Match still gives a cheap 304 for unchanged content) instead of 1y immutable. The /models/ location block (content-versioned by the face-api.js model release, rarely changes) can keep 1y immutable as-is -- this bead is scoped to the generic extension-based location block only.

Acceptance: curl -sI against app.js/config.js/styles.css on the live deployment (or a local docker-compose run) shows a short-TTL or no-cache Cache-Control, while the hashed main/vendors/runtime bundles and /models/ assets keep long immutable caching.

docker-app/public/monitoring.html is a complete, styled monitoring dashboard (dark theme, ~11.5KB) that fetches GET /health and renders it. It is never linked from anywhere -- not from docker-app/public/index.html, not from the README, not from any nav element. Confirmed via grep across docker-app/public and docker-app/src: zero references to monitoring.html outside itself. It ships in every deployed image (COPY --from=builder /app/public in docker-app/Dockerfile) but no visitor or operator has a way to discover it except by guessing the URL.

Separately, the /health endpoint it depends on is currently just a static string, defined directly in docker-app/nginx.conf:

location /health {
    access_log off;
    return 200 "OK\n";
    add_header Content-Type text/plain;
}

Meanwhile docker-app/public/health.json already exists with a richer shape (status, version, services, features flags for lite_mode/pro_mode/tensorflow_js/face_api_js) but is not what nginx actually serves at /health -- it is dead, unused JSON sitting in the image.

Work:
1. Add a footer or nav link to /monitoring.html from docker-app/public/index.html (or docker-app/src/index-wasm.js UI, depending on which survives ADR-001) so it is actually discoverable, e.g. "System status" link.
2. Change the /health nginx location to serve JSON matching (or replacing) the shape in docker-app/public/health.json, with real dynamic fields where feasible (at minimum a real timestamp instead of the literal string "dynamic"; ideally also whether models are present under /models via nginx returning file-existence-derived status, or leave that to client-side JS in monitoring.html to check /models/manifest.json separately if a static nginx response cannot do a dynamic check).
3. Update docker-app/public/monitoring.html rendering logic if the /health response shape changes.

Acceptance: monitoring.html is reachable via a visible link from the main app, and GET /health returns valid JSON (not the current plain-text "OK\n") that monitoring.html renders meaningfully.
